{
  "dom_id": "step-q344957",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193353/assets/4f3ec85e24c748cd99a11b27e2a6a60f04bbec7edaa07ed75a108e7f3ec01b93.png)\n\nThe graph of the first derivative $y={h}^{′}(x)$ of the function $h(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-5,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=4,$ as shown above. In **ascending order**, the $x$-values where the function $h(x)$ has a relative minimum are\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$ \n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${h}^{′}(x)$ is undefined, then we have a critical point.\n\n- If ${h}^{′}(x)>0,$ then $h(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${h}^{′}(x)<0,$ then $h(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nThe function $h(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=a$ if:\n\n- ${h}^{′}(a)=0$ or ${h}^{′}(x)$ is undefined at $x=a$, and\n\n- ${h}^{′}(x)$ changes sign from negative to positive (left to right) at $x=a.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $-5$ | $(-5,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${h}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $h$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nIn our case, we obtain relative minima at $x=-5$ and $x=4.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
